The final result is the digital root. For positive numbers, it is the remainder modulo 9, except that a remainder of 0 means 9. Writing the calculation as `1 + (num - 1) % 9` avoids that special case.

For example, `38` gives `2`, which matches the repeated sums `3 + 8 = 11` and `1 + 1 = 2`.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public int AddDigits(int num)
    {
        if (num == 0)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        return 1 + (num - 1) % 9;
    }
}
```

The input value is not changed. This is useful when the number can be large enough that repeatedly scanning its digits would be unnecessary.

Time complexity is O(1), and the extra space is O(1).

The number is reduced one digit sum at a time. For example, 987 becomes 24, then 6. Once the value has one digit, it is already the answer.

The loop also handles 0 correctly because the condition stops immediately.

